## Correlation test to identify highly correlated variables

```{r}
df <- meta
# Get only numerical columns
num_cols <- sapply(df, is.numeric)
numeric_df <- df[, num_cols]

# Prepare a dataframe to store the results
results <- data.frame(Variable1 = character(),
                      Variable2 = character(),
                      Correlation = numeric(),
                      P.value = numeric(),
                      stringsAsFactors = FALSE)

# Loop through all combinations of the numerical variables
for (vars in combn(names(numeric_df), 2, simplify = FALSE) ) {
  # Perform correlation test
  test <- cor.test(numeric_df[[vars[1]]], numeric_df[[vars[2]]])
  
  # Store the results
  results <- rbind(results, data.frame(Variable1 = vars[1],
                                        Variable2 = vars[2],
                                        Correlation = test$estimate,
                                        P.value = test$p.value))
}

cor_test_full_table <- results %>%
  filter(!grepl("tree|latitude|longitude|month|year", Variable1) & 
         !grepl("tree|latitude|longitude|month|year", Variable2)) 

cor_test_strong_corr_only <- cor_test_full_table %>%
  filter(Correlation < -.8 | Correlation > .8) %>%
  mutate(abscor = abs(Correlation)) %>%
  arrange(desc(abscor))
```

Examine the cor_test_strong_corr_only dataframe to identify strongly correlated variables in the metadata. These will have to be eliminated from the model to avoid overfitting by colinearity. For example we will remove altitude as it is strongly inversely correlated with average maximum temperatures, which has a slightly greater impact on beta-diversity as defined by the Pearson correlation tests. 


Note: There are a considerable number of correlations which may be due to climate factors affecting processes (ex. iron accumulation in low precipitation sites). In writing consider that climate can be driving changes in soil which in turn may be driving changes in microbial communities. Climate can also be driving changes in microbial communities which may be driving changes in soil processes (ex. decomposition leading to different CN rates.)


